#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/id #uuid "122fd33e-5ef4-5200-9c72-67f40f3b8ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8168" :question/problem "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that every quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ has an associated $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $L$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it's a $3 \\times 3$ matrix (not $2 \\times 2$).\n- $M$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Although it's a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, $M$ is not symmetric since $m_{12} \\neq m_{21}$.\n- $N$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since it is a $2 \\times 2$ symmetric matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$N$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f62dc8-396d-5f7f-8295-2e7b5d62bd78" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "d782c826-1111-5c83-98b6-220856d74b41" :question/math-academy-id "e-8169" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven the matrix $A$ above, evaluate the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ when $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ into the expression for $Q(\\mathbf{x})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x^{T}Ax \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 23\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 86\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161710 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "328b0b77-f789-581e-a119-9823297666ae" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Down a Quadratic Form Given Its Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "f3be44df-f059-5e92-b279-644fe1eb0985" :question/math-academy-id "e-8170" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which expression represents the corresponding quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the components of $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nEach entry $a_{ij}$ of $A$ corresponds to the term $a_{ij}x_i x_j$ in the expression of the quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{T}Ax &= -1x_{1}x_{1} + (1)x_{1}x_{2} + (1)x_{2}x_{1} + - 1x_{2}x_{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}x_{1} - x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo write down a quadratic form in terms of vector components, we compute the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{T}Ax &= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ x_{1} - x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= x_{1}(-x_{1} + x_{2}) + x_{2}(x_{1} - x_{2}) \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}x_{1} - x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&=-x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}x_{2} - x_{2}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161711 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb93c0ea-26d9-5190-9677-b8c7eb6f7ef1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "9ed75397-5920-5326-a5b9-7537e30f0a85" :question/math-academy-id "e-8171" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 & b \\\\ 0 & 2 & c \\\\ b & c & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}) = x_1^2 - 2x_1x_3 + 4x_2x_3 + 2x_2^2$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x_{1}^{2} - 2x_{1}x_{3} + 4x_{2}x_{3} + 2x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= 1x_{1}^{2} + 0x_{1}x_{2} + (-1)x_{1}x_{3} \\\\\n&= + 0x_{2}x_{1} + 2x_{2}^{2} + 2x_{2}x_{3} \\\\\n&= + (-1)x_{3}x_{1} + 2x_{3}x_{2} + 0x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must do the following splits:\n\n- we split the coefficient of ${\\color{blue}-2}x_1x_3$ between the entries $a_{13}=a_{31}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}-2}{2}=-1$, and\n- we split the coefficient of ${\\color{blue}4}x_2x_3$ between the entries $a_{23}=a_{32}=\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{2}=2$.\n\nSo, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1$, $b=-1$, and $c=2$. Finally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-1)+2 = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e62e458d-0724-5dbe-b448-b79404f279f9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices of Quadratic Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85efeaec-84c7-55b7-9a71-c2edc848f275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160741} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8af3e0-d091-59e8-87da-da408dbcdc88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160742} {:answer/id #uuid "b321d60d-ebbb-53b5-a0b8-16467541d489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160743} {:answer/id #uuid "01eda514-d471-5e0c-ba82-61a6ea36a328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$, $Q$, and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160744} {:answer/id #uuid "24560da7-da9d-5c33-